Thomas Pornin wrote:
> According to Doug Freyburger  <dfreybur@yahoo.com>:
>
>> 4) Fight monsters than you don't want to fight yourself.
>
> In my games, that's my main pet-using goal. I train the starting pet so
> that it can kill a few watchmen in Minetown, yielding gloves. Gloves are
> important for survival (when chickatrices and cockactrices begin to
> appear).

A horse is often powerful enough to take out the watchmen on the way
down.  Gloves are so important that when I lose my pet I might take out
at least one of them in games where I have not yet purchased protection.

> Killing off a watch captain, however, is a bit too much for a large dog
> or cat. It can be done, either with quite a bit of help from the RNG, or
> by healing the pet while it fights (beware, there is a slight penalty
> for that if you are chaotic). Healers are appropriate for that, since
> they start with healing spells and a stethoscope -- but healers are not
> especially interested in the long sword or silver saber that a watch
> captain may have.

I've lost lots of pets to the watch captain's weapon.  Pets attack based
on unarmed level not on weaponry.  If a pet attacks the watch captain
out of line of sight there's a high chance of "You feel sad for a
moment, but it passes".

> Another use is to get a pick-axe. For a lawful character, most dwarves
> will be peaceful.

The price of killing a peaceful is very low compared to the value of a
pickaxe or dwavish mattock.  Jackles are nutritious.

>> I have left pets in mine town after coming back up.  A pet who can
>> kill the mine town watch captain can get me a very good weapon, but it
>> can also kill shopkeepers and then attack and get killed by the mine
>> town priest ...
>
> Note that a cat or dog never goes higher than Mlvl 9, and thus never
> attacks a shopkeeper or a priest (unless you use conflict). It can be
> an issue with horses.

Right.  I was playing a knight when I lost my pet because it attacked
the minetown priest and I couldn't get it to leave before it died. 
Priests hit very hard.


